## Blocks

```mdl
api A-1: the array primitives
  signatures:
  - Array.make : Int -> a -> Array a
  - Array.length : Array a -> Int
  - Array.get : Array a -> Int -> a
  - Array.set : Array a -> Int -> a -> Array a
```

```mdl
perf ARRAY-1: a run of writes to the newest array is linear
  run: fun n => Array.Spec.fill n
  sizes: 100 | 200 | 400
  order: n
```

```mdl
law L-1: `make` builds an array of the length asked for
  theorem: Array.Spec.lengthMake
  evidence: proof
```

```mdl
law L-10: a write leaves the old array as it was
  theorem: Array.Spec.oldUnchanged
  evidence: test
```

```mdl
law L-2: every item of a made array is the value it was made with
  theorem: Array.Spec.getMake
  evidence: proof
```

```mdl
law L-3: `set` keeps the length
  theorem: Array.Spec.lengthSet
  evidence: proof
```

```mdl
law L-4: reading the index just written gives the value written
  theorem: Array.Spec.getSetSame
  evidence: proof
```

```mdl
law L-5: reading any other index gives what the old array had there
  theorem: Array.Spec.getSetOther
  evidence: proof
```

```mdl
law L-6: a second write to the same index replaces the first
  theorem: Array.Spec.setSetSame
  evidence: proof
```

```mdl
law L-7: writing back what is already there gives an equal array
  theorem: Array.Spec.setGetSame
  evidence: proof
```

```mdl
law L-8: two arrays are equal exactly when their items are
  theorem: Array.Spec.equalItems
  evidence: test
```

```mdl
law L-9: writes to different indices commute
  theorem: Array.Spec.setsCommute
  evidence: test
```

```mdl
req R-1: a write leaves every older array readable and unchanged, on every runtime
  by: Array.Spec.versionsIndependent | Array.Spec.oldUnchanged | Aliasing.allAgree
```

```mdl
req R-2: the kernels agree with the laws the prover assumes
  by: Array.Spec.lawsHoldOnKernels
```
